uel with one charge, and the deck panel shows which one you chose. from act ii, a new run can also encounter the bridge warden. inspect the opponent's starting spellbook before accepting the duel. a win earns an elite reward, a loss ends the expedition, and walking away lets you continue the route. open deck to check spell counts, creature stats, and the average base ether cost. when replacing a spell, select the outgoing card to compare it with the reward before confirming. press esc to step back from the deck or spell comparison. from the list of spells to replace, esc returns to the reward offers. the game saves when you enter a room, make a choice, or finish a combat action. if saving a battle result fails, choose retry save to keep the result before continuing. resume expedition restores the current room, including the battlefield, hands, and phase of an unfinished duel. older room-only saves resume from the duel's opening. if you already have a saved run, begin expedition asks before replacing it. choose keep saved expedition to return to the starter and seed you entered. replays etherlords ii duels can be saved and watched again (replay on the duel screen). after a completed local or network match, choose save duel replay on the final score screen. the game writes one replay per round and includes each round's starting decks and both players' combat actions. network replays open from the recording player's side. the engine reads the original replays and writes its own. see how a battle works."},
		{"key": "installing", "title": "Installing the game files", "category": "start", "html": "<h1>Installing the game files</h1><p>Demiurges reads the files of a game you own. It needs either game, or both: each game keeps its own rules, files and saves. Nothing is downloaded for you and no game content comes with the engine.</p><h2>Download the engine</h2><p>Get the package for your system from the <a href=\"https://github.com/TheWWWorm/Demiurges/releases\">releases page</a>, or <a href=\"wiki://browser\">play in the browser</a> without installing anything.</p><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>System</th><th>What to do</th></tr><tr><td>Windows (x86-64)</td><td>Extract the ZIP and run <code>Demiurges.exe</code>. SmartScreen may warn about an unrecognised app: choose <strong>More info → Run anyway</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>Linux (x86-64)</td><td>Extract the archive and run <code>Demiurges.x86_64</code>. If needed, run <code>chmod +x Demiurges.x86_64</code> first.</td></tr><tr><td>macOS</td><td>Extract the ZIP and open <code>Demiurges.app</code>. The app is not signed: right-click it and choose <strong>Open</strong>, or allow it under <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>Android 7+</td><td>Install the APK and allow your browser or file manager to install apps when asked.</td></tr></table><h2>Add a game</h2><p>At the first start the game files screen opens. Choose <strong>Etherlords I</strong> or <strong>Etherlords II</strong> at its top, then one of:</p><ul><li><strong>the game folder</strong>: the folder of your installed game, the one containing <code>Resources</code> and <code>Campaigns</code>;</li><li><strong>the GOG offline installer</strong> (<code>setup_etherlords*.exe</code>). It is <strong>not run</strong>: the engine unpacks the game files from it once and keeps them in its own data folder, so you do not need the installer afterwards;</li><li><strong>a data pack</strong> (<code>.elpack</code>, see below).</li></ul><p><strong>Steam copies</strong> are found automatically on Windows, Linux and macOS: at the first start the engine uses any it finds, and the game files screen offers <strong>Use the Steam copy</strong> for a game not set up yet.</p><p>Your choice is remembered. The original start-up videos play and then the <a href=\"wiki://main-menu\">main menu</a> opens. Repeat this for the other game to keep both.</p><h2>Data packs</h2><p>A data pack holds the files of one game in a single file. It is useful on a phone that has neither the installer nor the game folder: make the pack on your computer with the <code>prepare_game_data.py</code> script from the project (it needs Python 3) and copy it to the phone:</p><p><code>python3 prepare_game_data.py \"/path/to/Etherlords II\" Etherlords2.elpack</code></p><p class=\"note\">The installer and the data pack contain your own game files. Keep them to yourself.</p><h2>Adding, switching and removing games</h2><p><strong>Options › Remake settings › Game files…</strong> opens the same screen at any time to add a game, switch to another one or remove one. Removing a game keeps its saves and settings and never deletes an installed game folder.</p>", "search": "installing the game files demiurges reads the files of a game you own. it needs either game, or both: each game keeps its own rules, files and saves. nothing is downloaded for you and no game content comes with the engine. download the engine get the package for your system from the releases page, or play in the browser without installing anything. windows (x86-64) extract the zip and run demiurges.exe. smartscreen may warn about an unrecognised app: choose more info → run anyway. linux (x86-64) extract the archive and run demiurges.x86_64. if needed, run chmod +x demiurges.x86_64 first. macos extract the zip and open demiurges.app. the app is not signed: right-click it and choose open, or allow it under system settings → privacy & security. android 7+ install the apk and allow your browser or file manager to install apps when asked. add a game at the first start the game files screen opens. choose etherlords i or etherlords ii at its top, then one of: the game folder: the folder of your installed game, the one containing resources and campaigns; the gog offline installer (setup_etherlords.exe). it is not run: the engine unpacks the game files from it once and keeps them in its own data folder, so you do not need the installer afterwards; a data pack (.elpack, see below). steam copies are found automatically on windows, linux and macos: at the first start the engine uses any it finds, and the game files screen offers use the steam copy for a game not set up yet. your choice is remembered. the original start-up videos play and then the main menu opens. repeat this for the other game to keep both. data packs a data pack holds the files of one game in a single file. it is useful on a phone that has neither the installer nor the game folder: make the pack on your computer with the prepare_game_data.py script from the project (it needs python 3) and copy it to the phone: python3 prepare_game_data.py \"/path/to/etherlords ii\" etherlords2.elpack the installer and the data pack contain your own game files. keep them to yourself. adding, switching and removing games options › remake settings › game files… opens the same screen at any time to add a game, switch to another one or remove one. removing a game keeps its saves and settings and never deletes an installed game folder."},
		{"key": "browser", "title": "Playing in the browser", "category": "start", "html": "<h1>Playing in the browser</h1><p>The browser version runs at <a href=\"https://demiurges.wwworm.com/\">demiurges.wwworm.com</a>. There is nothing to install: open the page, press <strong>Play</strong> and give it your game files.</p><h2>Giving it your game files</h2><p>Choose one of:</p><ul><li>the GOG offline installer (<code>setup_etherlords*.exe</code>). It is not run: the browser unpacks the game files from it once and checks each one. This takes a few minutes and needs about 1.5 GB (Etherlords) or 3 GB (Etherlords II) of browser storage;</li><li>the folder of your installed game, in desktop browsers;</li><li>a <a href=\"wiki://installing\">data pack</a> (<code>.elpack</code>).</li></ul><p>Repeat this for the other game to keep both. Your files are <strong>never uploaded</strong>: the game reads them on your own device and keeps its copy in the browser's storage, so the next start is quick.</p><h2>Requirements</h2><ul><li>WebGL 2 and a desktop-class browser, or a recent phone with plenty of memory. Unpacking a large game needs a lot of memory for a short time; if it fails on a phone, use a data pack.</li><li>Intro and ending videos are not played in the browser yet.</li><li>Browsers can clear their storage. Keep your installer or pack, and remember that clearing the site's data also removes your saves.</li></ul><h2>This wiki</h2><p>The wiki you are reading is part of the same site at <a href=\"https://demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki/\">demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki</a> and is also built into the game: see <a href=\"wiki://about-wiki\">About this wiki</a>.</p>", "search": "playing in the browser the browser version runs at demiurges.wwworm.com. there is nothing to install: open the page, press play and give it your game files. giving it your game files choose one of: the gog offline installer (setup_etherlords.exe). it is not run: the browser unpacks the game files from it once and checks each one. this takes a few minutes and needs about 1.5 gb (etherlords) or 3 gb (etherlords ii) of browser storage; the folder of your installed game, in desktop browsers; a data pack (.elpack). repeat this for the other game to keep both. your files are never uploaded: the game reads them on your own device and keeps its copy in the browser's storage, so the next start is quick. requirements webgl 2 and a desktop-class browser, or a recent phone with plenty of memory. unpacking a large game needs a lot of memory for a short time; if it fails on a phone, use a data pack. intro and ending videos are not played in the browser yet. browsers can clear their storage. keep your installer or pack, and remember that clearing the site's data also removes your saves. this wiki the wiki you are reading is part of the same site at demiurges.wwworm.com/wiki and is also built into the game: see about this wiki."},
		{"key": "main-menu", "title": "Main menu, saves and settings", "category": "start", "html": "<h1>Main menu, saves and settings</h1><p>The main menu is the original game's menu. Under its buttons the remake adds <strong>Etherlords I</strong> and <strong>Etherlords II</strong>: click one to switch games, or <strong>Wiki</strong> to open this wiki. The game you played last opens at the next start. A game you have not added yet says <strong>not installed — add…</strong> and opens the <a href=\"wiki://installing\">game files screen</a> for it.</p><p>From the Wiki, open a creature's page to <a href=\"wiki://creatures\">play its attacks, death, and other animations with sound</a>.</p><h2>What the menu offers</h2><ul><li><strong>Etherlords</strong>: <strong>New Game</strong> (Duel, Campaign, Single Mission), <strong>Load Game</strong>, <strong>Options</strong>, <strong>Hall of Fame</strong>, <strong>Credits</strong> and <strong>Quit</strong>. See <a href=\"wiki://duel\">Duel</a>.</li><li><strong>Etherlords II</strong>: <strong>Single</strong> (Tutorial, Duel, Campaign, Mission), <strong>Multiplayer</strong> (<a href=\"wiki://duel\">network and hot seat duels</a>), <strong>Options</strong>, <strong>Encyclopedia</strong>, <strong>Credits</strong> and <strong>Quit</strong>.</li></ul><h2>Saves</h2><p>Saving and loading use the original screens. <strong>F5</strong> saves quickly and <strong>F9</strong> loads the quick save, on the map and in battle. Saves of the original games cannot be loaded; the engine keeps its own, separately for each game.</p><h2>Where saves and settings are kept</h2><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>System</th><th>Folder</th></tr><tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>%APPDATA%\\Godot\\app_userdata\\Etherlords Duel</code></td></tr><tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/Etherlords Duel</code></td></tr><tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>~/Library/Application Support/Godot/app_userdata/Etherlords Duel</code></td></tr><tr><td>Browser</td><td>the browser's storage for the site; clearing the site's data removes them</td></tr><tr><td>Android</td><td>the app's private storage; uninstalling the app removes them</td></tr></table><p>The folder keeps its earlier name, <code>Etherlords Duel</code>, so older saves continue to work.</p>", "search": "main menu, saves and settings the main menu is the original game's menu. under its buttons the remake adds etherlords i and etherlords ii: click one to switch games, or wiki to open this wiki. the game you played last opens at the next start. a game you have not added yet says not installed — add… and opens the game files screen for it. from the wiki, open a creature's page to play its attacks, death, and other animations with sound. what the menu offers etherlords: new game (duel, campaign, single mission), load game, options, hall of fame, credits and quit. see duel. etherlords ii: single (tutorial, duel, campaign, mission), multiplayer (network and hot seat duels), options, encyclopedia, credits and quit. saves saving and loading use the original screens. f5 saves quickly and f9 loads the quick save, on the map and in battle. saves of the original games cannot be loaded; the engine keeps its own, separately for each game. where saves and settings are kept windows %appdata%\\godot\\app_userdata\\etherlords duel linux ~/.local/share/godot/app_userdata/etherlords duel macos ~/library/application support/godot/app_userdata/etherlords duel browser the browser's storage for the site; clearing the site's data removes them android the app's private storage; uninstalling the app removes them the folder keeps its earlier name, etherlords duel, so older saves continue to work."},
		{"key": "controls", "title": "Controls", "category": "start", "html": "<h1>Controls</h1><p>The original controls work as in the games. These keys are the most useful.</p><h2>Keyboard and mouse</h2><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Key</th><th>Battle</th><th>Map</th></tr><tr><td>Left click</td><td>choose a spell, creature or target</td><td>move, talk, visit or attack</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>skip the current presentation</td><td>continue a conversation</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl+E (EL1) / Tab (EL2)</td><td>end the current phase</td><td>Ctrl+E ends the turn or day in either game</td></tr><tr><td>Alt+A</td><td>send all ready creatures to attack</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Alt+C</td><td>toggle auto combat</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>A / H (EL2)</td><td>show artifacts / spells in hand</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>E / G (EL2)</td><td>show enchantments / graveyard</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Esc</td><td>cancel targeting, or open the game menu</td><td>close a window, or open the game menu</td></tr><tr><td>Shift+F1</td><td>open this wiki</td><td>open this wiki (F1 also works)</td></tr><tr><td>F1–F4</td><td>choose an automatic camera view</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>F5 / F9</td><td>quick save / quick load</td><td>quick save / quick load (F8 also loads)</td></tr><tr><td>Right drag, wheel</td><td>turn and zoom the camera</td><td>turn and zoom the camera</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>reset the camera</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Page Up / Page Down</td><td>zoom in or out (EL2 in manual camera mode)</td><td>zoom in or out</td></tr><tr><td>Arrows</td><td>orbit or tilt in manual camera mode</td><td>EL1 pans, Ctrl+arrows rotate or tilt. EL2 Left/Right rotate and Up/Down zoom</td></tr><tr><td>W A S D</td><td>—</td><td>move the camera</td></tr><tr><td>H or Home</td><td>—</td><td>follow the selected hero</td></tr><tr><td>I / K</td><td>—</td><td>spellbook / skills in EL1. I opens inventory in EL2</td></tr><tr><td>N</td><td>—</td><td>end the turn in EL1 or open the notebook in EL2</td></tr></table><h2>Touch screens</h2><p>Phones, tablets and touch browsers have their own controls:</p><table data-columns=\"3\"><tr><th>Gesture</th><th>Battle</th><th>Map</th></tr><tr><td>Tap</td><td>as a left click</td><td>as a left click</td></tr><tr><td>Touch and hold</td><td>right click; on a button, its tooltip</td><td>right click (Etherlords II: the information panel while held)</td></tr><tr><td>Drag with one finger</td><td>turn the camera</td><td>move the map</td></tr><tr><td>Drag or twist with two fingers</td><td>turn and tilt the camera</td><td>turn and tilt the camera</td></tr><tr><td>Pinch</td><td>zoom</td><td>zoom</td></tr></table><p>A small bar at the top of the screen gives <strong>Back</strong> (Esc) everywhere, <strong>Camera</strong> (reset) in battle and <strong>Save</strong> / <strong>Load</strong> on the map. On Android the Back button also works as Esc.</p><h2>In the wiki</h2><p><strong>Alt+Left</strong> and <strong>Alt+Right</strong> go back and forward, <strong>Ctrl+F</strong> searches, and <strong>Esc</strong> or <strong>F1</strong> closes the wiki. The game waits while the wiki is open.</p><p>On a <a href=\"wiki://creatures\">creature page</a>, choose an action under the 3D model and press <strong>Play</strong> to preview it with its original sound when available.</p><h2>Console</h2><p>Press the backtick key under Esc or <strong>F10</strong> to open the console. Use <strong>Help</strong> to list the current commands, <strong>Tab</strong> to complete one, and <strong>Up</strong> or <strong>Down</strong> to browse history. In a local game, select <strong>Enable commands</strong> or enter <code>EtherRevelation</code>.</p><p>Type <code>help</code> followed by a space and press <strong>Tab</strong> to browse topics available on the current screen. Press <strong>Enter</strong> to read the selected topic.</p><p>On the adventure map, <code>player</code> lists the commanders. <code>view resources</code> shows your wallet. Use <code>view resources 0</code> for the neutral player, <code>view resources 1</code> for the first commander, and so on. <code>give Root 5</code> adds five roots in Etherlords I, and <code>give Herbs 5</code> adds five herbs in Etherlords II. Use <code>help give</code> to see the resource names for your edition.</p><p>In Etherlords I, <code>open_tomb</code> reveals the Ancient Tomb on the campaign map that contains it.</p><p>In battle, <code>view creature</code> lists both armies and their creature IDs. Use <code>view creature ID</code> for state and damage, or <code>view creature stats ID</code>, <code>view creature basestats ID</code>, and <code>view creature modifiers ID</code> to inspect stats and enchantments. <code>add enemy CODE</code> summons a creature for the opposing hero. Gameplay commands require a local game.</p><p>Use <code>view spells</code> to look up spell codes. Add a color, a spell class, or both to narrow the list. For example, <code>view spells blue enchantment</code> lists blue enchantments. Press <strong>Tab</strong> with the cursor in a filter to complete it.</p>", "search": "controls the original controls work as in the games. these keys are the most useful. keyboard and mouse left click choose a spell, creature or target move, talk, visit or attack space skip the current presentation continue a conversation ctrl+e (el1) / tab (el2) end the current phase ctrl+e ends the turn or day in either game alt+a send all ready creatures to attack — alt+c toggle auto combat — a / h (el2) show artifacts / spells in hand — e / g (el2) show enchantments / graveyard — esc cancel targeting, or open the game menu close a window, or open the game menu shift+f1 open this wiki open this wiki (f1 also works) f1–f4 choose an automatic camera view — f5 / f9 quick save / quick load quick save / quick load (f8 also loads) right drag, wheel turn and zoom the camera turn and zoom the camera r reset the camera — page up / page down zoom in or out (el2 in manual camera mode) zoom in or out arrows orbit or tilt in manual camera mode el1 pans, ctrl+arrows rotate or tilt. el2 left/right rotate and up/down zoom w a s d — move the camera h or home — follow the selected hero i / k — spellbook / skills in el1. i opens inventory in el2 n — end the turn in el1 or open the notebook in el2 touch screens phones, tablets and touch browsers have their own controls: tap as a left click as a left click touch and hold right click; on a button, its tooltip right click (etherlords ii: the information panel while held) drag with one finger turn the camera move the map drag or twist with two fingers turn and tilt the camera turn and tilt the camera pinch zoom zoom a small bar at the top of the screen gives back (esc) everywhere, camera (reset) in battle and save / load on the map. on android the back button also works as esc. in the wiki alt+left and alt+right go back and forward, ctrl+f searches, and esc or f1 closes the wiki. the game waits while the wiki is open. on a creature page, choose an action under the 3d model and press play to preview it with its original sound when available. console press the backtick key under esc or f10 to open the console. use help to list the current commands, tab to complete one, and up or down to browse history. in a local game, select enable commands or enter etherrevelation. type help followed by a space and press tab to browse topics available on the current screen. press enter to read the selected topic. on the adventure map, player lists the commanders. view resources shows your wallet. use view resources 0 for the neutral player, view resources 1 for the first commander, and so on. give root 5 adds five roots in etherlords i, and give herbs 5 adds five herbs in etherlords ii. use help give to see the resource names for your edition. in etherlords i, open_tomb reveals the ancient tomb on the campaign map that contains it. in battle, view creature lists both armies and their creature ids. use view creature id for state and damage, or view creature stats id, view creature basestats id, and view creature modifiers id to inspect stats and enchantments. add enemy code summons a creature for the opposing hero. gameplay commands require a local game. use view spells to look up spell codes. add a color, a spell class, or both to narrow the list. for example, view spells blue enchantment lists blue enchantments. press tab with the cursor in a filter to complete it."},
		{"key": "options", "title": "Options", "category": "start", "html": "<h1>Options</h1><p><strong>Options</strong> opens the original Options screen of each game, from the main menu or the game menu. Each game keeps its own settings.</p><h2>Original options</h2><p>These work as in the games: autosave, faster map movement, animated objects, ambient sounds, hero voices, the tutorial, spells in <a href=\"wiki://automatic-combat\">automatic combat</a>, the <strong>Automatic camera</strong> and <strong>Automatic camera dynamics</strong> (how often close-ups happen), what is shown in battle (<strong>Combat phases</strong>, <strong>Power/toughness values</strong>, <strong>Abilities icons</strong>, <strong>Attacking/blocking order</strong>), camera speeds, gamma and reversed stereo. A few options are kept but have no effect yet.</p><h2>Remake settings</h2><p><strong>Remake settings…</strong> is available on the Options screen and in the Etherlords II Esc menus on maps and in battle.</p><h3>Graphics</h3><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Renderer</td><td><strong>Compatibility</strong> (the default) runs on more computers. <strong>Forward+ (Vulkan)</strong> is needed for path tracing. <strong>Restart now</strong> restarts the game with the chosen renderer.</td></tr><tr><td>Path-traced lighting</td><td>Real shadows, sky light, light bounced between surfaces, and light from fire and spells, on maps and in battles. Needs Forward+ and a graphics card with ray tracing. It costs about 2–3 ms a frame on a fast card. <strong>F7</strong> switches it during play. The line under it says whether your computer can use it.</td></tr><tr><td>Show light only</td><td>Shows the traced light without textures (<strong>Shift+F7</strong>).</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-aliasing</td><td>Smoother edges in 3D: automatic, off, 2× or 4×.</td></tr><tr><td>Sharpening</td><td>Sharpens 3D textures. Choose off, light (default), or strong.</td></tr><tr><td>Shadows</td><td>Shadows of figures in battles and of trees, rocks and buildings on maps.</td></tr><tr><td>Soft shadows</td><td>Softer shadow edges. Off at first.</td></tr><tr><td>Terrain detail</td><td>Subtle texture and relief on map ground and fine grain on battle ground. Off at first.</td></tr><tr><td>Enhanced water</td><td>Reflected scenery, animated ripples, and soft shoreline foam on the adventure map. Bog water has gentler ripples. In Etherlords II, rain adds rings to the water while Map weather is enabled. Off at first.</td></tr><tr><td>Map weather</td><td>Rain, snow and other weather on adventure maps.</td></tr><tr><td>Combat blood</td><td>Blood in battles.</td></tr></table><h3>Audio</h3><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Music, Ambience, Effects</td><td>Volumes. Ambience is the remake's own background sound of maps and battles.</td></tr></table><h3>Interface</h3><table data-columns=\"2\"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Movies</td><td>Off, original size or fit to the screen.</td></tr><tr><td>Map tooltips on hover</td><td>Etherlords II map tooltips without a right click.</td></tr><tr><td>Remake helper overlay</td><td>The remake's readouts, hints and shortcut buttons over the original screens. Off at first.</td></tr><tr><td>Frame rate counter</td><td>Frames per second in the top right corner. Off at first.</td></tr><tr><td>Discord activity</td><td>Shows in the Discord desktop app which game you play and whether you are in the menus, on a map or in a battle. Nothing else is shared. On at first.</td></tr></table><p>On the <strong>Hotkeys</strong> tab, choose an edition and select a shortcut to change it. Press a key or key combination, or press <strong>Esc</strong> to cancel. <strong>Reset defaults</strong> restores the selected edition’s shortcuts for battle, maps and shops.</p><p>At the bottom, <strong>Export log…</strong> saves a log with system information for a <a href=\"wiki://troubleshooting\">problem report</a>, and <strong>Game files…</strong> adds, switches or removes a game: see <a href=\"wiki://installing\">Installing the game files</a>.</p><p>Leave the heavier settings (path tracing, soft shadows, terrain detail, 4× anti-aliasing) off on devices where they slow the game down.</p>", "search": "options options opens the original options screen of each game, from the main menu or the game menu. each game keeps its own settings. original options these work as in the games: autosave, faster map movement, animated objects, ambient sounds, hero voices, the tutorial, spells in automatic combat, the automatic camera and automatic camera dynamics (how often close-ups happen), what is shown in battle (combat phases, power/toughness values, abilities icons, attacking/blocking order), camera speeds, gamma and reversed stereo. a few options are kept but have no effect yet. remake settings remake settings… is available on the options screen and in the etherlords ii esc menus on maps and in battle. graphics renderer compatibility (the default) runs on more computers. forward+ (vulkan) is needed for path tracing. restart now restarts the game with the chosen renderer. path-traced lighting real shadows, sky light, light bounced between surfaces, and light from fire and spells, on maps and in battles. needs forward+ and a graphics card with ray tracing. it costs about 2–3 ms a frame on a fast card. f7 switches it during play. the line under it says whether your computer can use it. show light only shows the traced light without textures (shift+f7). anti-aliasing smoother edges in 3d: automatic, off, 2× or 4×. sharpening sharpens 3d textures. choose off, light (default), or strong. shadows shadows of figures in battles and of trees, rocks and buildings on maps. soft shadows softer shadow edges. off at first. terrain detail subtle texture and relief on map ground and fine grain on battle ground. off at first. enhanced water reflected scenery, animated ripples, and soft shoreline foam on the adventure map. bog water has gentler ripples. in etherlords ii, rain adds rings to the water while map weather is enabled. off at first. map weather rain, snow and other weather on adventure maps. combat blood blood in battles. audio music, ambience, effects volumes. ambience is the remake's own background sound of maps and battles. interface movies off, original size or fit to the screen. map tooltips on hover etherlords ii map tooltips without a right click. remake helper overlay the remake's readouts, hints and shortcut buttons over the original screens. off at first. frame rate counter frames per second in the top right corner. off at first. discord activity shows in the discord desktop app which game you play and whether you are in the menus, on a map or in a battle. nothing else is shared. on at first. on the hotkeys tab, choose an edition and select a shortcut to change it. press a key or key combination, or press esc to cancel. reset defaults restores the selected edition’s shortcuts for battle, maps and shops. at the bottom, export log… saves a log with system information for a problem report, and game files… adds, switches or removes a game: see installing the game files. leave the heavier settings (path tracing, soft shadows, terrain detail, 4× anti-aliasing) off on devices where they slow the game down."},
		{"key": "differences", "title": "Differences from the originals", "category": "remake", "html": "<h1>Differences from the originals</h1><p>Demiurges follows the rules of the original games. It runs on modern systems and changes only what this page lists.</p><h2>Added</h2><ul><li>Both games in one program, switched from the main menu, each with its own rules, saves and settings.</li><li>Windows, Linux, macOS, Android and the browser; touch controls.</li><li><a href=\"wiki://options\">Remake settings</a>: soft shadows, terrain detail, sharpening, anti-aliasing and more.</li><li>The automatic camera can be interrupted by moving the camera yourself.</li><li>This wiki, in English and Russian, in the game and on the web.</li></ul><h2>Not available</h2><ul><li>GameSpy and full strategic multiplayer. Native builds offer <a href=\"wiki://duel\">network duels and a strategic preview</a>.</li><li>Loading saves of the original games. Original Etherlords II replays can be watched.</li><li>Intro and ending videos in the browser.</li></ul><h2>Not yet exact</h2><ul><li>Computer opponents follow the original's approach but are not an exact copy of its decisions.</li><li>The darkening of a few large spells, the timing of some ability effects and Etherlords II map particles are approximations.</li><li>The remake's own screens follow the language of your game. A few rare error messages and the battle log are still in English.</li></ul><h2>Kept on purpose</h2><p>Some behaviour of the originals looks like a mistake but is kept because the games work that way:</p><ul><li>The Etherlords skills <strong>Ethereal Attack</strong> and <strong>Ethereal Defense</strong> do nothing.</li><li>Random battle events of Etherlords never happen, as in the original.</li><li>Several Etherlords global spells only cost upkeep.</li><li>As in the originals, computer players never answer alliance or trade offers.</li></ul><p>See <a href=\"wiki://troubleshooting\">Problems and reporting</a> for known gaps.</p>", "search": "differences from the originals demiurges follows the rules of the original games. it runs on modern systems and changes only what this page lists. added both games in one program, switched from the main menu, each with its own rules, saves and settings. windows, linux, macos, android and the browser; touch controls. remake settings: soft shadows, terrain detail, sharpening, anti-aliasing and more. the automatic camera can be interrupted by moving the camera yourself. this wiki, in english and russian, in the game and on the web. not available gamespy and full strategic multiplayer. native builds offer network duels and a strategic preview. loading saves of the original games. original etherlords ii replays can be watched. intro and ending videos in the browser. not yet exact computer opponents follow the original's approach but are not an exact copy of its decisions. the darkening of a few large spells, the timing of some ability effects and etherlords ii map particles are approximations. the remake's own screens follow the language of your game. a few rare error messages and the battle log are still in english. kept on purpose some behaviour of the originals looks like a mistake but is kept because the games work that way: the etherlords skills ethereal attack and ethereal defense do nothing. random battle events of etherlords never happen, as in the original. several etherlords global spells only cost upkeep. as in the originals, computer players never answer alliance or trade offers. see problems and reporting for known gaps."},
		{"key": "troubleshooting", "title": "Problems and reporting", "category": "remake", "html": "<h1>Problems and reporting</h1><p>Demiurges is still in development. These are the things you are most likely to notice.</p><h2>Known gaps</h2><ul><li>A few rare error messages and the battle log are still shown in English.</li><li>Computer opponents follow the original's approach but do not copy its decisions exactly. As in the original, they never answer alliance or trade offers.</li><li>The campaigns are tested screen by screen and system by system, not yet by playing every chapter from start to finish.</li><li>Network duels use the host's address. The browser build supports local hot seat.</li><li>Touch controls have only been tried in testing, not yet on many real phones. The interface keeps the original layout, so on a small phone text and buttons are small.</li><li>Intro and ending videos are not played in the browser yet.</li></ul><h2>If the game does not start</h2><ul><li>Check that you chose the folder that contains <code>Resources</code> and <code>Campaigns</code>, or the GOG <strong>offline</strong> installer. See <a href=\"wiki://installing\">Installing the game files</a>.</li><li>In the browser, make sure the page has enough storage and memory; on a phone, try a data pack.</li><li>On Windows and macOS, allow the unsigned app as described in <a href=\"wiki://installing\">Installing the game files</a>.</li></ul><h2>Reporting a problem</h2><p>Report problems on the <a href=\"https://github.com/TheWWWorm/Demiurges/issues\">issues page</a>. Say which game and which system you use, and what you did before the problem appeared.</p>", "search": "problems and reporting demiurges is still in development. these are the things you are most likely to notice. known gaps a few rare error messages and the battle log are still shown in english. computer opponents follow the original's approach but do not copy its decisions exactly. as in the original, they never answer alliance or trade offers. the campaigns are tested screen by screen and system by system, not yet by playing every chapter from start to finish. network duels use the host's address. the browser build supports local hot seat. touch controls have only been tried in testing, not yet on many real phones. the interface keeps the original layout, so on a small phone text and buttons are small. intro and ending videos are not played in the browser yet. if the game does not start check that you chose the folder that contains resources and campaigns, or the gog offline installer. see installing the game files. in the browser, make sure the page has enough storage and memory; on a phone, try a data pack. on windows and macos, allow the unsigned app as described in installing the game files. reporting a problem report problems on the issues page. say which game and which system you use, and what you did before the problem appeared."},
